# Line endings that matter for the curl-able installers (Task B):
# raw.githubusercontent.com serves the COMMITTED BLOB as-is — not a
# checkout — so what lands in the object database is exactly what users
# pipe into bash/cmd.
#
# install.cmd MUST be CRLF in the blob: cmd.exe's label scanner breaks
# on LF-only files ("cannot find the batch label"). `text eol=crlf`
# would NOT work — that still stores LF in the blob and only expands on
# checkout. The blob must carry the CRLF bytes itself, so: disable
# normalization (-text) and keep the working file CRLF.
install.cmd -text
# install.ps1: CRLF in the blob as well — PowerShell handles LF fine,
# but Notepad-culture consistency + zero benefit to mixing.
install.ps1 -text
# install.sh: must be LF in the blob (bash chokes on CRLF shebangs);
# normalization is on but it is already LF, so the blob is byte-identical.
install.sh text eol=lf
# Shell scripts generally must stay LF.
*.sh text eol=lf

